Here’s a concise way to wire everything up in **one** statement – creating the Redux‑style store, passing your custom hooks, and adding both the logging middleware *and* the extra “myMiddleWare” enhancer:

```javascript
// One‑liner initialization of the React state manager
const myAppState = manageReactState({
  /* Create the underlying store */
  store: createStore(
    reducersMap,                     // Reducer map
    initialStateObject,              // Initial state snapshot
    applyMiddleware(                 // Compose middlewares/enhancers
      loggerMiddleware,               // Your logger
      applyMiddleware("myMiddleWare") // Additional enhancer
    )
  ),

  /* Context used to expose the store down the component tree */
  context: AppContext,

  /* Hook helpers exposed to consumer components */
  hooks: {
    useStateSelectorHook,            // Selector hook
    useDispatchActionHook           // Dispatch hook
  }
});
```

> **What happens here?**
>
> - `createStore(...)` builds the core state container from your reducers, starting state, and composed middlewares/​enhancers.
> - `appContext` supplies a React context so child components can consume the store without prop drilling.
> - The two custom hooks (`useStateSelectorHook` & `useDispatchActionHook`) give components convenient ways to read state slices and fire actions respectively.
> - Finally, `manageReactState()` bundles all three pieces together into whatever internal API it exposes (e.g., returning `{Provider, Consumer}` pairs, or simply exposing the configured store).

Feel free to replace `AppContext`, `createStore`, or other identifiers with whichever libraries / patterns you’re already using!

